<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajivika</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ajivikas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajivika was an ascetic religious movement of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, led in the 5th century BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a>, a contemporary and rival of the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>. Its followers held a strict determinism, believing that fate (niyati) governs everything. The movement received caves in the Barabar Hills from the Maurya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> but later declined, and its teachings are known only from Buddhist and Jain sources.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajivika.jpg" alt="Ajivika" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_the_Buddha_-_Parinirvana_-_Kushan_dynasty,_late_2nd_to_early_3rd_century_AD,_Gandhara,_schist_-_Freer_Gallery_of_Art_-_DSC05119.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
